3. Longest substring without repeating characters.

   APPROACH: Sliding Window

   Java 21 CODE:

   import java.util.*;

   public class Main {
       public static int longestSubstring(String s) {
           Map<Character, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
           int left = 0;
           int max = 0;

           for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
               char ch = s.charAt(right);

               if (map.containsKey(ch) && map.get(ch) >= left) {
                   left = map.get(ch) + 1;
               }

               map.put(ch, right);
               max = Math.max(max, right - left + 1);
           }

           return max;
       }

       public static void main(String[] args) {
           System.out.println(longestSubstring("abcabcbb")); // 3
           System.out.println(longestSubstring("bbbbb"));   // 1
           System.out.println(longestSubstring("pwwkew"));  // 3
       }
   }

   EXPLANATION:
   1. Maintain a sliding window containing unique characters.
   2. Use a HashMap to store the latest index of each character.
   3. If a duplicate appears inside the current window, move the left pointer.
   4. Update the maximum window length at every iteration.

   Time Complexity: O(n)
   Space Complexity: O(min(n, character set size))

